1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,956
Lesson 0.6.

2
00:00:02,336 --> 00:00:05,193
Fourier analysis on finite abelian groups.

3
00:00:06,317 --> 00:00:08,994
Shor's algorithm and the thermodynamic

4
00:00:08,994 --> 00:00:13,221
order-finding-by-diffusion
algorithm, which we study in unit

5
00:00:13,221 --> 00:00:18,223
seven, share one language: Fourier
analysis over the integers modulo r.

6
00:00:18,603 --> 00:00:22,448
Shor amplifies Fourier peaks
with quantum interference;

7
00:00:22,448 --> 00:00:25,664
diffusion damps every
Fourier mode except one.

8
00:00:26,044 --> 00:00:29,056
To understand both we
need the characters of a

9
00:00:29,056 --> 00:00:32,397
finite group and their key
property, orthogonality.

10
00:00:33,517 --> 00:00:36,884
The integers modulo r
are the arithmetic of an

11
00:00:36,884 --> 00:00:40,033
r-hour clock: after r
minus one comes zero.

12
00:00:40,413 --> 00:00:44,471
Let's place those r positions on the
unit circle of the complex plane.

13
00:00:44,851 --> 00:00:49,168
We choose a frequency k:
position x is sent to the point

14
00:00:49,168 --> 00:00:52,791
k times x steps of r
further around the circle.

15
00:00:53,171 --> 00:00:56,396
With frequency one, we
go around once as we

16
00:00:56,396 --> 00:00:59,847
traverse the clock; with
frequency two, twice.

17
00:01:00,227 --> 00:01:02,812
Each frequency is a wave
living on the clock.

18
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:09,665
That wave is a character:
χ_k(x) = e^(2πikx/r).

19
00:01:10,045 --> 00:01:13,627
Its essential property
is that it turns sums

20
00:01:13,627 --> 00:01:17,046
into products: χ_k(x + y) = χ_k(x) χ_k(y).

21
00:01:17,426 --> 00:01:21,060
Adding on the clock is equivalent to
composing rotations on the circle.

22
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:24,527
There are exactly r
distinct characters, one for

23
00:01:24,527 --> 00:01:27,100
each frequency from zero to r minus one.

24
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:30,941
The zero-frequency character
is constant and equal to one.

25
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:34,477
Now let's add up all the
values of a character.

26
00:01:34,857 --> 00:01:36,730
Each value is a unit vector.

27
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:40,232
If we place them head to tail, they form a

28
00:01:40,232 --> 00:01:43,576
regular polygon that
closes: the sum is zero.

29
00:01:43,956 --> 00:01:47,512
Only when the frequency
is a multiple of r do all

30
00:01:47,512 --> 00:01:50,995
vectors point the same
way and the sum equals r.

31
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:55,244
Orthogonality follows: the
inner product of two different

32
00:01:55,244 --> 00:01:59,385
characters is zero, and that of
a character with itself is r.

33
00:02:00,517 --> 00:02:03,718
Since the characters are
r orthogonal vectors in

34
00:02:03,718 --> 00:02:06,519
an r-dimensional space, they form a basis.

35
00:02:06,899 --> 00:02:10,757
Any function on the clock can be
written as a combination of waves.

36
00:02:11,137 --> 00:02:19,877
The coefficients are the discrete Fourier
transform, f̂(k) = Σ f(x) χ̄_k(x),

37
00:02:20,257 --> 00:02:22,600
and the function is
recovered by adding the

38
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:24,944
waves with those weights
and dividing by r.

39
00:02:25,324 --> 00:02:29,341
The animation builds the bar function
by adding one wave at a time.

40
00:02:30,617 --> 00:02:33,352
Why does this matter for
thermodynamic computing?

41
00:02:33,732 --> 00:02:36,881
A random walk on the
cycle is a convolution,

42
00:02:36,881 --> 00:02:39,959
and characters diagonalize
any convolution.

43
00:02:40,339 --> 00:02:45,992
For the lazy walk, which stays with
probability one half and moves to each

44
00:02:45,992 --> 00:02:51,645
neighbor with one quarter, the eigenvalue
of mode k is ½ (1 + cos(2πk/r)).

45
00:02:52,025 --> 00:02:56,990
Watch the probability of a walker starting
at zero: it spreads around the ring.

46
00:02:57,370 --> 00:03:00,467
In the Fourier representation,
each mode decays by

47
00:03:00,467 --> 00:03:03,441
its own factor, and only
the zero mode survives.

48
00:03:03,821 --> 00:03:08,734
This distribution is the discrete heat
kernel, and its value at the origin, the

49
00:03:08,734 --> 00:03:12,964
return probability, is the average
of the powers of the eigenvalues.

50
00:03:14,083 --> 00:03:20,307
Let's compute a small transform with r =
4 and the function one, two, zero, one.

51
00:03:20,687 --> 00:03:26,013
The characters of four take the
values one, i, minus one and minus i.

52
00:03:26,393 --> 00:03:29,798
The zero-frequency
coefficient is the sum, four.

53
00:03:30,178 --> 00:03:32,565
The frequency-one
coefficient is one minus i.

54
00:03:32,945 --> 00:03:37,377
The frequency-two one is minus two, and
the frequency-three one, one plus i.

55
00:03:37,757 --> 00:03:42,792
Let's check Parseval's identity: the sum
of squares of the function is six, and

56
00:03:42,792 --> 00:03:47,252
that of the transform is twenty-four,
which divided by four gives six.

57
00:03:48,383 --> 00:03:50,832
In unit seven we will use all of this.

58
00:03:51,212 --> 00:03:53,772
Shor uses a quantum Fourier transform to

59
00:03:53,772 --> 00:03:56,332
reveal the period of a modular function.

60
00:03:56,712 --> 00:04:02,363
The diffusion algorithm obtains the same
period from the return probability of a

61
00:04:02,363 --> 00:04:07,589
random walk, which is exactly an average
of powers of Fourier eigenvalues.

62
00:04:08,717 --> 00:04:12,178
Every finite abelian group is
a product of cyclic groups, and

63
00:04:12,178 --> 00:04:15,186
its characters are products
of the ones we saw today.

64
00:04:15,566 --> 00:04:20,823
In unit seven we will apply this machinery
to Cayley graphs, and in the diffusion

65
00:04:20,823 --> 00:04:25,822
models of unit nine we will see that
diffusion erases high frequencies first.

66
00:04:26,950 --> 00:04:30,449
Frequent mistakes: forgetting
the conjugate in the inner

67
00:04:30,449 --> 00:04:33,948
product, forgetting the
one-over-r factor in the inverse

68
00:04:33,948 --> 00:04:37,009
transform, and confusing
frequency with position.

69
00:04:37,389 --> 00:04:41,464
Remember too that the sum
of the roots of unity equals

70
00:04:41,464 --> 00:04:45,086
r whenever r divides k,
not only when k is zero.

71
00:04:46,217 --> 00:04:49,522
In summary: the characters
of the integers modulo

72
00:04:49,522 --> 00:04:52,288
r are waves that turn sums into products.

73
00:04:52,668 --> 00:04:56,029
They are orthogonal because
the roots of unity sum to zero.

74
00:04:56,409 --> 00:05:01,034
They form a basis, the Fourier
transform, and they diagonalize random

75
00:05:01,034 --> 00:05:05,124
walks on the cycle, whose modes
decay with their eigenvalues.

76
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:10,960
To practice: compute the sum of
the roots of unity for any k.

77
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:13,448
Check the homomorphism property.

78
00:05:13,828 --> 00:05:16,807
Compute two four-point Fourier transforms.

79
00:05:17,187 --> 00:05:22,692
And find the eigenvalues and the gap of
the lazy walk on an eight-state cycle.
